    exemplar_studios: ['Egyptian State Radio, Maspero (Cairo)', 'Sono Cairo / Misrphon (Egyptian state label)', 'Radio Baghdad (al-Chalghi al-Baghdadi maqam broadcasts)', 'Radio Tehran (Golha orchestral programmes, 1956-1979)'],
    note: 'A state-monopoly broadcast culture in which national radio orchestras and their star vocalists were captured live-to-mono in large government halls rather than commercial rooms. At Egyptian State Radio\'s Maspero building and the Sono Cairo state label, a single U47-school tube condenser stood in front of the massed-strings firqa behind a reciter or singer such as Umm Kulthum, feeding German V72-school tube broadcast pres and a tube broadcast desk whose only dynamics stage was Telefunken U73b-style limiting, with no channel EQ and the reverberant hall itself as the sole ambience. The identical Telefunken-equipped signal path recurred at Radio Baghdad\'s al-Chalghi al-Baghdadi maqam broadcasts and Radio Tehran\'s Golha programmes, cutting full-track 15 ips masters that reached listeners through AM transmission and state-label vinyl. The result is the dry-but-cavernous, EQ-free, wholly tube-broadcast sound of the mid-century Arab and Persian orchestral canon — a chain defined by broadcast infrastructure rather than the pre-1948 consoleless disc-cutting it is often mistaken for.'
