    exemplar_studios: ['Studio Rachid & Fethi, Tlemcen (Rachid Baba Ahmed)', 'Disco Maghreb, Oran (Boualem Benhaoua)'],
    note: 'Producer-run 16-track rooms in western Algeria — Rachid Baba Ahmed\'s Studio Rachid & Fethi in Tlemcen and Boualem Benhaoua\'s Disco Maghreb in Oran — built pop-rai by grafting a Yamaha DX7 and drum machine onto derbouka and gasba, with a single melismatic vocal cut in one take on a handheld SM58-school dynamic. The desk is a mid-tier UK solid-state inline board (Soundcraft TS24/3200 school) run hot into dbx VCA compression and graphic EQ, dressed with spring or cheap-digital reverb and chorus rather than any orchestral hall. Crucially the chain has no vinyl stage: masters went straight to industrial cassette duplication, the format that carried rai out of the Oranais bars and across the Maghreb and its diaspora. The lineage is a label-owned commercial production line — closer to a Casablanca cassette factory than to a Western multitrack studio — with the producer serving as arranger, engineer, and distributor at once.'
