    exemplar_studios: ['Sout El Hob, Cairo (Ahmed Adaweya\'s label)', 'Koliphone / Azoulay Brothers, Jaffa', 'Reuveni Brothers, Tel Aviv (Zohar Argov)'],
    note: 'Two parallel Eastern-Mediterranean cassette economies — Cairo\'s shaabi labels such as Ahmed Adaweya\'s Sout El Hob and the Israeli Mizrahi houses of the Azoulay Brothers\' Koliphone in Jaffa and the Reuveni Brothers in Tel Aviv — converged on an identical low-budget chain. Live bands cut one or two takes in a single room, the melisma-forward vocalist working close on a rugged stage dynamic while electric organ, accordion and electric guitar stood in for the classical takht and a derbouka sat forward in the balance. Signal passed through console input strips on a small colored solid-state desk with no compression, graphic EQ for quick tone-shaping and spring reverb as the only ambience, then was dubbed hot straight to cassette. The medium is the culture: masters were high-speed duplicated in the tens of thousands and sold from street kiosks and bus-station stalls, so the saturated, lo-fi cassette itself — not any mastering polish — defines the sound.'
