    exemplar_studios: ['HMV Dum Dum Calcutta (Gramophone Company of India)', 'All India Radio Delhi/Bombay studios', 'HMV Madras', 'AVM Studios Madras (Carnatic sessions)'],
    note: 'The recorded sound of the North and South Indian art-music canon on LP: the Gramophone Company of India\'s Dum Dum plant outside Calcutta and the All India Radio broadcast studios cut soloist-centric sessions in which a single U47-school tube condenser (or a pencil condenser) captured voice, sitar, sarod or veena while the tanpura drone and tabla accompaniment were balanced by physical placement in a dry broadcast room rather than mixed. Signal ran through V72-school German tube broadcast pres into Siemens/Telefunken-lineage tube desks with no compression, no channel EQ and no reverb or effects — the deliberately unadorned "HMV sound." Full-track 15 ips tape was the enabling medium: it lifted the roughly four-minute constraint of the shellac side and made the twenty-minute raga exposition of Ravi Shankar, Ali Akbar Khan and M.S. Subbulakshmi possible at all, with AVM Studios in Madras extending the same one-or-two-mic idiom to the Carnatic repertoire and HMV Calcutta housing the Tagore-song and Baul sessions.'
