    components: { mic: 'dynamic_mic', pre: 'tube_pre', console: 'clean', comp: 'comp_none', eq: 'eq_none', medium: 'walkman_dictaphone' },
    exemplar_studios: ['AnTrop — Andrei Tropillo\'s studio, Leningrad House of Young Pioneers', 'Moscow kvartirnik apartment-concert tape circuit (Vysotsky, Okudzhava, Galich)', 'Zvukozapis kiosk dubbing points (copy-generation distribution)'],
    note: 'A censorship-evading home-tape culture rather than a studio: a single cheap Soviet dynamic mic fed straight into the tube electronics of a consumer reel-to-reel deck — Yauza, Dnepr or Mayak running at 9.5–19 cm/s — with no console, compression or EQ. Andrei Tropillo\'s AnTrop, improvised inside Leningrad\'s House of Young Pioneers, cut the canonical magnitoalbomy of Aquarium and Kino, while the Moscow kvartirnik circuit captured Vysotsky, Okudzhava and Galich live in apartments; both reached listeners only through Zvukozapis dubbing kiosks. Because the music was heard through third- and fourth-generation copies, the accumulated hiss, wow and dropout of serial re-recording are inseparable from the sound — a historian-recognized recording tradition with no Western analog, defined by its degraded distribution medium as much as by its one-mic capture chain.'
