    exemplar_studios: ['Supraphon Domovina Studio, Prague (plus Rudolfinum Dvořák Hall rig)', 'Polskie Nagrania Muza, Warsaw (with Polish Radio studios)', 'Qualiton/Hungaroton Rottenbiller Street studio, Budapest', 'Electrecord, Bucharest', 'Balkanton / Bulgarian National Radio Studio 1, Sofia'],
    note: 'The shared recording apparatus of the non-Soviet Eastern bloc, where a single state label per country pressed everything from concert-hall classical to village folk through one broadcast-heritage chain. That chain was unified by an East German equipment lineage — Neumann Gefell tube large-diaphragm condensers (UM57/M582), V72/V76-school tube broadcast pres and desks, vari-mu limiting, passive program EQ, EMT 140 plate, and Hungarian Mechlabor STM decks at 15 ips — run in purpose-built label and radio rooms rather than churches or halls, yielding a drier, close-miked, export-graded sound. Supraphon\'s Domovina studio in Prague, Hungaroton\'s Rottenbiller Street rooms in Budapest, Electrecord in Bucharest and Balkanton\'s Radio Studio 1 in Sofia shared a QC-driven mastering discipline aimed at hard-currency export, cutting everything from Ančerl\'s Czech Philharmonic to the Bulgarian Radio women\'s choir of \'Le Mystère des Voix Bulgares\'. The result is distinct from Melodiya\'s distant church-hall miking: a tube-console, plate-reverb studio aesthetic applied uniformly across magyar nóta Gypsy orchestras, Romanian lăutari tarafs and Polish sung poetry alike.'
