    note: 'The defining chain of postwar musical ethnography: a battery-powered Nagra III or IV-S quarter-inch reel machine carried into prison yards, villages and forest clearings, fed by one or two pencil condensers or a passive ribbon — frequently an ORTF or mid-side pair — through the recorder\'s own portable preamp, with no console, compression or equalization between microphone and tape. Alan Lomax\'s penitentiary and Global Jukebox expeditions, Moses Asch\'s Smithsonian Folkways, and Charles Duvelle and Simha Arom\'s Ocora sessions for Radio France all share this single-perspective, un-mixed aesthetic in which the room tone of the location is part of the document rather than a defect; Constantin Brăiloiu\'s UNESCO anthology and the Nonesuch Explorer series extended the same lone-folklorist workflow across Java, the Caucasus and Central Africa. The sound is defined by what the chain omits — no studio, no overdubs, no processing — leaving only a single mic position, magnetic tape, and the acoustic of wherever the performance happened to occur.'
