    note: 'The earliest surviving recordings of much of the world\'s oral music were captured not in a studio but on a spring-wound portable phonograph carried into the field, its sound gathered mechanically through a horn and diaphragm and inscribed directly into soft wax — no electricity, no microphone amplification, no console, and nothing between the horn and the cylinder to compress or equalize. The institutional lineage runs from the comparative-musicology laboratories of the Berlin Phonogramm-Archiv (Hornbostel and Stumpf) and the Vienna Phonogrammarchiv to the salvage ethnography of the Smithsonian Bureau of American Ethnology, where Frances Densmore and Jesse Walter Fewkes documented Native repertoire, and to the folk-collecting expeditions of Bartók and Kodály across the Hungarian and Romanian countryside. The medium is the signature: a narrow-band, high-surface-noise document of roughly two to three minutes per cylinder, the wax degrading with each playback, which set both the fidelity ceiling and the archival stakes of the entire corpus. Because the rig is purely acoustic, the chain carries no preamp, console, compression, or EQ — the horn-and-cylinder pairing is the whole signal path.'
