    note: 'A self-contained recording vehicle rather than a fixed room: the truck parks outside the venue, takes a mic split from the stage feed plus a dedicated audience array, and captures a single-pass live performance to multitrack for later studio mixdown. The archetypal rig is the Rolling Stones Mobile Studio, whose onboard Helios console and pre-amps — later paralleled by Record Plant Remote, Wally Heider Remote and Le Mobile — gave the 1970s live-album canon (Get Yer Ya-Ya\'s Out, Exile on Main St.) its transformer-colored stage sound. Bus compression on the way to 2-inch 30 ips tape tames unpredictable stage dynamics, while the hall\'s own ambience and audience noise, sweetened with hall reverb back at the studio, define the recognizable \'live LP\' character. The workflow is remote and one-time — no isolation booths and no overdub safety net — with monitor-mix bleed and audience mics standing in for the controlled separation of a flagship control room.'
